Distributed preprocessing
=========================

Run a build under `pump` to distribute preprocessing as well as compilation:

    pump make -j"$(distcc -j)"

The build must invoke distcc as its compiler, as with ordinary distcc. Pump
starts a pool of persistent include analyzers for the build and stops it when
the command exits. It does not require build-system hooks or special handling of generated
headers. Without pump, ordinary client-side preprocessing remains the default.
For Clang C-family sources, the client rewrites includes while preserving macros
and the worker finishes preprocessing. This preserves macro-sensitive diagnostics
without requiring application headers on workers. The worker must support the
requested target, options and source language. Dependency files are
produced on the client.

Clang HIP and direct AMDGPU C/C++/OpenCL jobs use full client preprocessing,
even under pump. HIP compilation follows the selected driver's action graph,
preprocessing each host and GPU translation unit with its own frontend options.
TCP workers perform code generation and device linking; LLVM assembles the
images on the client. ROCm device bitcode and intermediate objects are shared
through the input cache. Workers need Clang and its offload tools, but do not
need the client's HIP headers or ROCm libraries.
The selected Clang executable is preserved for LLVM bitcode/LTO compatibility;
host `native` CPU options and `--offload-arch=native` are resolved on the client.
TCP workers send progress frames while jobs run or await execution, so long
GPU compilations do not expire solely because no result is ready yet.
The Portage jobs hook sets the CMake C, C++ and HIP compiler launchers to `distcc`
when distribution is enabled, unless the user or package supplies a launcher.
This also covers projects compiling HIP through the C++ language with `-x hip`.
Other CMake builds can set `CMAKE_HIP_COMPILER_LAUNCHER=distcc` (or the C++
equivalent) before their first configure.

With DISTCC_BROKER set, clients request remote preprocessing through the existing
broker. Adaptive scheduling and the TCP connection pool are reused. These jobs
reserve a local preprocessing slot only during input analysis. Without a broker, configure remote
hosts with the `,cpp,lzo` options.

Pump obtains the pool size from `distcc --show-analyzer-slots`: available logical
CPUs capped by CPU affinity and ancestor cgroup quotas. Analysis and ordinary
local preprocessing share occupied leases. Ordinary preprocessing keeps its
configured slot limit; native analysis can use the available CPUs and publishes
its observed resident plus frontend peak memory. One initial request calibrates
demand before parallel scans are admitted using that peak and the existing
memory headroom policy. Imports happen before forking so immutable library pages
are shared. Memory budgets and pressure are checked
in the requesting client's cgroup. Busy clients wait for capacity
without switching preprocessing mode, including `-E` and `--scan-includes`.
Homogeneous broker clients finish analysis before reserving a remote worker.
Each analyzer owns its cwd and caches. The supervisor replaces failed analyzers
and retains their published input snapshots until pump shuts down, so an
in-progress transfer cannot lose its inputs during recovery.

The client expands GNU-style `@response` files with LLVM's argument parser before
classifying the job. Empty CMake module maps and nested response files therefore
work with pump; native options and input paths inside them receive the same
treatment as command-line arguments. Actual module generation still stays local.

The include server calls libclang-cpp directly through a resident Python C++
extension. Building pump requires LLVM 22 or 23 development headers, libclang-cpp,
libLLVM and a C++17 compiler; set LLVM_CONFIG to the matching llvm-config if it
is not on PATH or beside clang. Clang release strings do not gate analysis;
the frontend reports unsupported inputs through its normal diagnostics.
The Clang and LLVM libraries must also use the same assertion/debug ABI settings.
For Clang jobs, the canonical preprocessor discovers includes and existence probes without
parsing declarations or generating code. Its input buffers are reused for transfer.
Each request gets a fresh FileManager to avoid stale negative lookup caches.
Each analyzer retains only the preceding request's input bytes and module-syntax
checks. Unchanged file identities and timestamps allow reuse. Each path is
observed once during discovery and validated again before publication. Python
receives read-only views of the native input buffers; these retain their contents
even after the next scan replaces the cache, avoiding per-job header copies.
Ordinary macro expansion is retained because side effects such as __COUNTER__
and _Pragma can change subsequent include conditions.

An analyzer also retains one in-memory cache of the current common initial
include group. On its second use, Clang's native PCH writer saves the
preprocessor state without parsing header declarations. Later translation units
with the same compiler options, working/source directories and prefix restore
that state and preprocess only the remaining source. Clang's lexer determines
prefix boundaries; headers and macros are not interpreted in Python. A shared
prefix can be reused even when subsequent includes differ between source files.
The cache includes the native macro/conditional/include state, __COUNTER__,
dependency snapshots and positive/negative header lookups. File identity or
timestamp changes and newly created or removed headers invalidate it before
reuse. Snapshots are validated again before publication.

Headers using invocation-dependent builtins or user push_macro/pop_macro
are scanned normally because their context is not fully
represented by this PCH state. A pop_macro in the remaining source also triggers
a full scan, including restoration of stacks created by compiler predefines.
Preprocessing errors also use the normal scanner;
cache creation never turns a valid job into a rejected job. Only the current
include group is retained per analyzer, and it is released when the analyzer
exits. `pump` statistics report PREAMBLES hits and builds. This cache accelerates
Clang input discovery; worker compilation and the native GCC scanner still
preprocess their original inputs.

Clang pump jobs use the installed `distcc-clang` worker helper. Its virtual
filesystem resolves absolute include operands and existence probes against the
transferred files while preserving the original main-file identity, including
static-function profile names. Target and language defaults are inferred from
the requested driver name. The worker library executes the requested compilation
without requiring identical driver release metadata. Clang IR profile generation (`-fprofile-generate`) also uses this helper
for locally preprocessed input. Prepared TCP jobs transfer Clang profile-use,
sample-profile, memory-profile and profile-remapping inputs. The compiler driver
in `clang_driver.so` discovers these files and discards unused linker inputs
before preprocessing changes the command's language. Profile files share verified,
read-only cache inodes across jobs; relative paths resolve against the client
working directory. Build the helper with the selected `LLVM_CONFIG` and install
it on clients together with the updated worker helper.
GCC profile generation and profile use remain local because their additional file contracts are not
part of the compilation RPC.

GCC jobs invoke the selected GCC frontend with a dependency-collection plugin.
The module accesses GCC's own libcpp input state, including existence-only probes
that ordinary dependency output omits. GCC performs condition evaluation, macro
expansion, token pasting and header search; Python does not interpret C/C++.
One full GCC preprocessing process runs per request. Dependency-only mode is
insufficient: ordinary macro expansion can affect later include conditions
through __COUNTER__. Collected records use bounded buffered output; diagnostic
macro-expansion provenance is disabled during collection only.
PCH and embed directives require local preprocessing. Compiler module and
dependency-scanner flags require local compilation because their sidecar outputs
are not part of the remote job protocol. GCC workers use
the same module's libcpp search callback to resolve absolute include operands
and existence probes inside the transferred input tree. GCC absolute operands
with parent-directory traversal still require local preprocessing.
GCC also reports each search directory's native system-header class. Workers
preserve ordinary, C++ system and extern-C system directories rather than
converting all paths to -isystem, which changes C++ header attributes.

Build the module using the exact source of the selected GCC, including any
downstream changes to libcpp, and that compiler's installed plugin headers:

    python3 include_server/build_gcc_scan.py --gcc gcc --source /path/to/gcc-source \
        --output build/gcc

Install the resulting target/version/distcc_gcc_scan.so under
LIBDIR/distcc/gcc-scan, and register a symlink to it as distcc_gcc_scan.so in
the directory reported by that GCC's -print-file-name=plugin. Both clients and
workers need the module. Repeat for each GCC used by pump. For an x86-hosted cross
compiler, build the module with an x86 C++ compiler. When building a module to
run on another architecture, use --cxx and --plugin-headers for that architecture's
native compiler. The source and installed cpplib.h must agree, and GCC checks
the module's version/ABI before loading it. DISTCC_GCC_SCAN_LIBRARY overrides the
module path for development tests. A missing or incompatible module produces an
analysis failure; it does not silently switch to another parser.

Native GCC scans also use the request-local negative lookup cache. Directory
changes discard the input manifest and repeat the same frontend without that
cache. DISTCC_CPP_LOOKUP=0 disables this optimization; DISTCC_CPP_LOOKUP_LIBRARY
overrides the installed cache library for development tests.

Compressed source/header files are cached. The compiler scans every request and
its observed input identities are checked before publishing the manifest. On supported local Linux
filesystems, directory notifications select which negative lookups and ancestor
identities need rechecking. Failed header candidates reuse these observations,
including directories that the compiler skipped; positive files retain fresh
metadata checks. Other filesystems and unavailable or exhausted
notification watches retain full metadata checks. Header creation, modification, removal and symlink
replacement invalidate cached inputs. A new generation holds new compressed files;
previously issued manifests keep their old files until pump exits. If cached
observations change during a native scan, the cache generation is retired
and the request is restarted once. Repeated invalidation or changed current
file contents causes local preprocessing instead. Build
dependencies must still prevent writes to an input during its compilation.
An aborted analysis does not publish a partial manifest.

The worker receives the raw source and snapshots of application and system
headers, runs the compiler's preprocessor and compiles the result. Default
include paths are sent in compiler order, including Clang's system-header class
and GCC's implicit headers. The worker disables its own default search paths
and reads the client's private input tree. Explicit include options retain
their search order and header classification. Existing search directories and
their symlinks are recreated even when the job reads no headers from them, so
the worker does not introduce missing-directory warnings. Missing directories
and probes stay missing even when a worker has additional headers installed.

System headers use the same compression and immutable input cache as application
headers. They do not need to match the worker installation or be retransmitted
on every job. The generated objects must be compatible with the client's target
ABI and linker. This transfers
the required header closure, not libraries or a complete sysroot for linking.

For Gentoo, source `packaging/gentoo/pump.bashrc` (installed by the overlay as
`/usr/share/distcc/portage-pump.bashrc`) from `/etc/portage/bashrc`, and call
`distcc_pump_start` from `pre_src_compile` and `distcc_pump_stop` from
`post_src_compile`. The start helper registers failure cleanup within the compile
phase. Enable selected packages with `DISTCC_PUMP=1` in their `package.env`
environment file. The helper preserves Portage's compiler wrapper search order.

Standalone GCC and Clang `-E` calls with a source file are also distributed.
Standard output, `-o` output files, explicit `-MF` dependencies, exit status and
partial standard output on preprocessing errors are returned to the client.
The worker's temporary path is removed from preprocessor output. Standard output
is staged until the response finishes, so a dropped connection followed by a
local retry does not duplicate output. Upgrade clients and workers together to
use remote `-E`.

Stdin input, dependency-only `-M`/`-MM`, `-E -MD` without explicit `-MF`,
PCH/modules, frontend overrides and `#embed` remain local.
Computed `__has_include` and `__has_include_next` operands are supported.
Existence probes participate in include lookup invalidation even
when no include directive follows. Other include-analysis failures fall back to
ordinary distcc; standalone preprocessing falls back to a local compiler when
DISTCC_FALLBACK permits it. DISTCC_TESTING_INCLUDE_SERVER=1 turns analysis fallback
into an error for validation.

DISTCC_CLIENT_TMP selects the compressed-input storage directory. Deep absolute
paths are supported when the variable is shared by pump and its distcc clients.
Storage for old generations is reclaimed at pump shutdown. Files are compressed
once per generation but still transferred per job; worker file caching is not
part of this protocol. Speedups depend on preprocessing cost, include-analysis
cost, network throughput and the number of independent build jobs.

`make preprocess-check` exercises real GCC/Clang preprocessing through distccd,
mutable and generated headers, dependency output, compiler errors, interrupted
responses, broker admission and pooled connections.
